type: framework
namespace: EU
version: "2024/1689"
anchor_version: ">=6.0.0"
maintainer: "Anchor Core"
opt_in: true
source: "EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation EU 2024/1689)"
source_url: "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj/eng"
source_date: "August 1, 2024"
credit: >
  Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 of the European Parliament and of the
  Council laying down harmonised rules on artificial intelligence.
  Published in the Official Journal of the European Union, L series, 2024.

rules:

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # CHAPTER II — PROHIBITED AI PRACTICES (Article 5)
  # Fines up to €35 million or 7% of worldwide annual turnover (Article 99)
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - id: "EU-ART05-A"
    name: "Prohibited AI — Subliminal Deception & Behavior Distortion"
    original_id: "Article 5(1)(a)"
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 27, 28, 29"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Strictly prohibits AI systems that deploy subliminal, manipulative,
      or deceptive techniques with the objective or effect of materially
      distorting a person's behavior, impairing informed decision-making,
      and causing or being likely to cause significant harm.

  - id: "EU-ART05-B"
    name: "Prohibited AI — Exploitation of Vulnerabilities"
    original_id: "Article 5(1)(b)"
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 30, 31"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Prohibits AI systems that exploit any vulnerabilities of a person or a
      specific group of persons due to age, disability, or socio-economic
      situation, to materially distort behavior in a manner causing harm.

  - id: "EU-ART05-C"
    name: "Prohibited AI — Social Scoring Systems"
    original_id: "Article 5(1)(c)"
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 31, 32"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Prohibits AI systems that evaluate or classify natural persons over
      time based on social behavior or personal characteristics, leading to
      detrimental or unfavorable treatment in unrelated social contexts.

  - id: "EU-ART05-E"
    name: "Prohibited AI — Untargeted Facial Image Scraping"
    original_id: "Article 5(1)(e)"
    maps_to: "PRV-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 33, 34, 35"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Strictly prohibits AI systems that create or expand facial recognition
      databases through the untargeted scraping of facial images from the
      internet or CCTV footage.

  - id: "EU-ART05-F"
    name: "Prohibited AI — Emotion Recognition in Workplace & Schools"
    original_id: "Article 5(1)(f)"
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 36, 37"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Prohibits AI systems used to infer emotions of natural persons in
      workplace and educational institution settings, except for medical
      or safety reasons.

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # CHAPTER III — HIGH-RISK AI SYSTEMS (Articles 6 – 27)
  # Fines up to €30 million or 6% of worldwide annual turnover (Article 99)
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - id: "EU-ART06"
    name: "High-Risk Classification & Profiling Derogation Cancellation"
    original_id: "Article 6(3)"
    maps_to: "LEG-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 46, 47, 48, 49, 50, 51, 52, 53"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      AI systems falling under Annex III are classified as high-risk. While
      operators may claim derogations for narrow tasks, any system performing
      profiling of natural persons is ALWAYS classified as high-risk without exception.

  - id: "EU-ART08"
    name: "Compliance with Mandatory High-Risk System Requirements"
    original_id: "Article 8"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recital 54"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      High-risk AI systems must comply with mandatory requirements established
      in Chapter III Section 2 (Articles 9 through 15) before being placed on
      the market or put into service.

  - id: "EU-ART09"
    name: "Risk Management System — Continuous Lifecycle"
    original_id: "Article 9"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 54, 55, 56"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      A documented, continuous risk management system must be maintained across
      the entire AI lifecycle. System must identify known and foreseeable risks,
      evaluate risk severity, adopt management measures, and continuously test
      the system before market placement and in production.

  - id: "EU-ART10"
    name: "Data and Data Governance — Bias & Lineage Controls"
    original_id: "Article 10"
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 57, 58, 59, 111"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Training, validation, and testing datasets for high-risk AI systems must
      be subject to strict data governance practices, including data provenance,
      representativeness, error mitigation, and mandatory bias detection/mitigation.

  - id: "EU-ART11"
    name: "Technical Documentation — Annex IV Pipeline"
    original_id: "Article 11"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    recitals: "Recital 60"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Technical documentation must be drawn up before the system is placed on the
      market or put into service, containing all information listed in Annex IV
      to demonstrate compliance to national competent authorities.

  - id: "EU-ART12"
    name: "Automated Record-Keeping & Logging"
    original_id: "Article 12"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: provenance
    recitals: "Recitals 61, 62"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      High-risk AI systems must technically enable automatic logging of events
      throughout their operational lifecycle to ensure operational traceability,
      post-hoc monitoring, and failure reconstruction.

  - id: "EU-ART13"
    name: "Transparency & Information Provision to Deployers"
    original_id: "Article 13"
    maps_to: "ETH-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 63, 64"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      High-risk AI systems must be designed to ensure operations are transparent
      to deployers, providing clear instructions for use, capabilities, performance
      limitations, accuracy metrics, and human oversight mechanisms.

  - id: "EU-ART14"
    name: "Human Oversight — Overrides & Kill-Switches"
    original_id: "Article 14"
    maps_to: "ETH-003"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 65, 66, 67"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      High-risk AI systems must be designed to enable effective human oversight,
      including deterministic intervention levers, automation bias countermeasures,
      and kill-switch override buttons that allow humans to interrupt or stop operations.

  - id: "EU-ART15"
    name: "Accuracy, Robustness and Cybersecurity Gates"
    original_id: "Article 15"
    maps_to: "SEC-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 68, 69, 70, 71"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      High-risk AI systems must achieve appropriate levels of accuracy, structural
      robustness, and cybersecurity. Systems must be resilient against prompt injection,
      data poisoning, model evasion, and unauthorized host command execution.

  - id: "EU-ART16"
    name: "Obligations of Providers of High-Risk AI Systems"
    original_id: "Article 16"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    recitals: "Recitals 72, 73, 74"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Providers must ensure compliance with all technical requirements, draw up
      technical documentation, operate a quality management system, conduct conformity
      assessments, register in the EU AI database, and affix CE marking.

  - id: "EU-ART17"
    name: "Quality Management System Implementation"
    original_id: "Article 17"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 75, 76"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Providers of high-risk AI systems must establish and document a quality
      management system ensuring compliance, including post-market monitoring,
      resource management, and systemic testing procedures.

  - id: "EU-ART18"
    name: "Documentation Preservation — 10-Year Retention Rule"
    original_id: "Article 18"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: provenance
    recitals: "Recital 76"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Providers must keep technical documentation, quality management records,
      and conformity decisions at the disposal of national competent authorities
      for 10 years after the AI system is placed on the market.

  - id: "EU-ART19"
    name: "Automatically Generated Log Preservation — 6-Month Rule"
    original_id: "Article 19"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: provenance
    recitals: "Recitals 61, 62, 76"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Providers and deployers of high-risk AI systems must retain automatically
      generated logs for a minimum period of 6 months, unless sectoral law or
      contractual obligations require a longer retention period.

  - id: "EU-ART20"
    name: "Corrective Actions & Serious Incident Duty"
    original_id: "Article 20"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 77, 78"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Providers who consider that a high-risk AI system is not in conformity must
      immediately take necessary corrective actions to bring it into conformity,
      withdraw it, or recall it, and inform national competent authorities.

  - id: "EU-ART25"
    name: "Responsibilities Along the AI Value Chain"
    original_id: "Article 25"
    maps_to: "SUP-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 80, 81, 82"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Any distributor, importer, deployer or third party that modifies a high-risk
      AI system, changes its intended purpose, or places it under their own name
      is considered a provider and inherits full Article 16 provider obligations.

  - id: "EU-ART26"
    name: "Obligations of Deployers of High-Risk AI Systems"
    original_id: "Article 26"
    maps_to: "ETH-003"
    obligation_type: audit
    recitals: "Recitals 83, 84, 85, 86, 87, 88, 89"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Deployers must implement human oversight measures, monitor system operations for
      anomalies, suspend use when serious risk arises, inform providers of incidents,
      and retain automatically generated logs for at least 6 months.

  - id: "EU-ART27"
    name: "Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment (FRIA)"
    original_id: "Article 27"
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    recitals: "Recitals 90, 91, 92, 93, 94"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Prior to deploying a high-risk AI system, deployers that are bodies governed
      by public law or private entities providing public services must perform an
      assessment of the impact on fundamental rights.

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # CHAPTER IV — TRANSPARENCY FOR AI CONTENT & GPAI (Articles 50 – 56)
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - id: "EU-ART50"
    name: "Transparency & Synthetic Content Watermarking"
    original_id: "Article 50"
    maps_to: "AGT-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 131, 132, 135"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Providers of AI systems generating audio, image, video or text content must
      ensure synthetic outputs are marked in a machine-readable format and detectable
      as artificially generated or manipulated (watermarking & deepfake labeling).

  - id: "EU-ART53"
    name: "General-Purpose AI (GPAI) Model Technical Documentation"
    original_id: "Article 53"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    recitals: "Recitals 150, 151, 152"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Providers of General-Purpose AI (GPAI) models must draw up technical documentation,
      provide information to downstream AI providers integrating the model, and respect
      EU copyright law directives.

  - id: "EU-ART55"
    name: "GPAI Models with Systemic Risk — Red-Teaming & Evaluation"
    original_id: "Article 55"
    maps_to: "SEC-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    recitals: "Recitals 153, 154, 155"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Providers of GPAI models with systemic risk must perform model evaluation,
      conduct adversarial red-teaming, assess and mitigate systemic risks, and track
      serious incidents and corrective measures.

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # CHAPTER IX & XII — POST-MARKET MONITORING & PENALTIES (Articles 72 – 99)
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - id: "EU-ART72"
    name: "Post-Market Monitoring System Setup"
    original_id: "Article 72"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: audit
    recitals: "Recital 160"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Providers must establish and document a post-market monitoring system proportional
      to the nature of the AI technologies, actively collecting data on system performance.

  - id: "EU-ART73"
    name: "Reporting of Serious Incidents to National Authorities"
    original_id: "Article 73"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    recitals: "Recital 161"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Providers must report any serious incident involving a high-risk AI system to the
      relevant national competent authority immediately, and no later than 15 days after
      becoming aware of the incident.

  - id: "EU-ART99"
    name: "Penalties & Administrative Fines"
    original_id: "Article 99"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    recitals: "Recitals 173, 174, 175, 176"
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Violations of Article 5 prohibited practices carry administrative fines up to
      €35 million or 7% of total worldwide annual turnover. Violations of high-risk
      requirements carry fines up to €30 million or 6%.
